/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  kinematicCloudProperties - oilWaterAirParticleFoam   (FIXED VERSION)
  SMALL case (oilWaterAirParticle). Mineral particles (rho = 2500 kg/m3) in a
  water/oil/air VOF field.

  Force models
  ────────────
  gravity          : MANDATORY. In OpenFOAM the gravity/buoyancy force on a
                     parcel is applied ONLY if it is listed here - see
                     GravityForce::calcNonCoupled:
                         Su = mass*g*(1 - rho_c/rho_p)
                     Nothing in KinematicParcel::calcVelocity adds g by itself.
                     Without this entry the particles are effectively weightless
                     (that is what happened in the delivered
                     oilWaterAirParticle_large run: the two lower parcels just
                     drifted sideways for 0.32 s instead of settling).
                     Note that constant/g is (0 -9.81 0), i.e. already correct,
                     and that this force term ALREADY contains buoyancy through
                     rho_c, so there is no double counting with pressureGradient.
  sphereDrag       : Schiller-Naumann drag.
  pressureGradient : rho_c/rho_p * DUc/Dt - wake suction.
  virtualMass      : added mass, Cvm = 0.5 for a sphere.

  Scale check for the SMALL case (5.6 x 7 mm, dx = 14 um, d_b = 0.7 mm)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Particle diameter is set to 14 um, i.e. exactly one cell, not the previous
  35 um. Reason: the Lagrangian cloud is a POINT-particle formulation - the
  parcel is a point that samples the carrier field at its own position, and the
  drag correlation assumes the undisturbed carrier velocity. That assumption
  requires d_p <= dx. At 35 um the parcel spanned 2.5 cells, which a thesis
  reviewer will flag, and the interpolated "undisturbed" velocity it feels is
  really its own displaced flow.

  Consequence you must be aware of and state explicitly:

      d_p = 14 um -> Stokes settling velocity  0.16 mm/s
                  -> particle response time    tau_p = 27 us
                  -> Stokes number St = tau_p * U_b / d_b = 0.005

  With St ~ 5e-3 the particles are essentially passive tracers. They cannot be
  captured by inertia, and they cannot outrun the bubble either. So at this
  length scale any "trapping" you observe is caused by

     (a) recirculation in the bubble wake carrying tracers upward, or
     (b) an interface-attachment force, which no standard OpenFOAM
         ParticleForce provides - it needs a custom model (see README).

  This is not a bug in the setup - it is a scale-invariance problem. Bubble
  dynamics scale with Eo = rho*g*d_b^2/sigma and Mo; particle inertia scales
  with St. Shrinking the whole geometry by 0.07 kept Eo small (0.069) but
  destroyed St. For St = O(0.1-1) you need the 10 mm bubble with 250-500 um
  particles (St = 0.17-0.69). Worth one paragraph in the thesis.

  If you accept the point-particle violation, set the value back to 3.5e-5
  (settling 1.0 mm/s, St = 0.032) - still a tracer, just a less rigorous one.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    object      kinematicCloudProperties;
}

solution
{
    active          true;
    coupled         no;         // one-way coupling; see note below
    transient       yes;
    cellValueSourceCorrection no;
    maxCo           0.3;

    sourceTerms
    {
        schemes { U explicit 1; }
    }

    interpolationSchemes
    {
        rho         cell;
        U           cellPoint;
        muc         cell;
        p           cell;
        DUcDt       cell;
    }

    integrationSchemes { U analytical; }
}

constantProperties
{
    rho0            2500;       // [kg/m3] mineral particle density
}

subModels
{
    particleForces
    {
        sphereDrag;
        gravity;                        // mandatory - see note above
        pressureGradient { U U; }
        virtualMass      { Cvm 0.5; }
    }

    injectionModels
    {
        model1
        {
            type            manualInjection;
            SOI             0;
            massTotal       0;          // ignored for parcelBasisType fixed
            parcelBasisType fixed;
            nParticle       1;
            positionsFile   "kinematicCloudPositions";
            U0              (0 0 0);

            sizeDistribution
            {
                type fixedValue;
                fixedValueDistribution
                {
                    value   1.4e-5;     // 14 um = 1 cell (point-particle limit)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    dispersionModel             none;
    patchInteractionModel       standardWallInteraction;
    stochasticCollisionModel    none;
    surfaceFilmModel            none;

    standardWallInteractionCoeffs
    {
        type    rebound;
        e       0.9;
        mu      0.1;
    }
}

cloudFunctions {}

